            </figure>
          </section>

          <section id="obstetric-safety">
            <SectionShare id="obstetric-safety" title="Obstetric Safety" />
            {/* T9: safety framing prose. */}

            <p>The clearest safety signal in the RRM outcomes data is two straightforward percentages. In the Boyle 2025 head-to-head cohort, 4.0% of RRM singleton babies were born preterm. The CDC benchmark for IVF singleton births that same year was 11.8%. That is roughly two-thirds fewer early births in the RRM group <a href="/library/restorative-reproductive-medicine-rrm-outcomes-compared-to-in-vitro-fertilization-rec4qqhafqb8stlnd/">[source]</a>. Across all RRM pregnancies, the preterm rate was 6.5%, against 14.4% in the SART IVF benchmark: more than half the rate <a href="/library/restorative-reproductive-medicine-rrm-outcomes-compared-to-in-vitro-fertilization-rec4qqhafqb8stlnd/">[source]</a>. RRM twins are rare to begin with. In the Boyle 2018 post-IVF-failure cohort, only one of 74 RRM live births was a twin pregnancy (1.4%) <a href="/library/healthy-singleton-pregnancies-from-restorative-reproductive-medicine-rrm-after-f-recior3akxtg2a6ya/">[source]</a>.</p>
            <p>The comparison papers for ART-treated populations report the data differently. Neither published a per-group preterm percentage for singleton pregnancies; they published adjusted odds ratios. The plain reading: for single babies, the adjusted odds of an early birth were about 3 times higher after ART versus women who never used fertility treatment (OR 3.28, 95% CI 1.74 to 6.20) <a href="/library/fertility-treatments-and-adverse-perinatal-outcomes-in-a-population-based-sampli-rec9u2zrqqgyuipxq/">[source]</a>. Among women who were already subfertile, the adjusted odds of an early birth were about 4 times higher after IVF and about 3 times higher after IUI, compared with subfertile women who went untreated that cycle (IVF OR 4.24, 95% CI 2.05 to 8.77; IUI OR 3.17, 95% CI 1.40 to 7.19) <a href="/library/fertility-treatments-and-the-risk-of-preterm-birth-among-women-with-subfertility-recpxh63cqyktiu3x/">[source]</a>.</p>
            <p>The mechanism is straightforward: RRM achieves conception through a single naturally-conceived embryo, so multiple gestations are rare. Multiple gestations drive the majority of preterm births and NICU admissions in IVF populations. When conception follows restoration of the underlying cause rather than embryo transfer, that driver largely disappears. The data reflect that reality consistently: RRM birth outcomes, particularly on prematurity and multiples, look substantially better than IVF benchmarks across the published cohorts.</p>

            <figure class="chart-figure">
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/obstetric-safety.svg?v=3" alt="Two-panel chart. Panel A reads as plain percentages, how many babies out of 100: RRM single-baby preterm 4.0 percent versus the CDC IVF benchmark 11.8 percent (RRM about two-thirds lower), RRM all-pregnancy preterm 6.5 percent versus the SART IVF benchmark 14.4 percent (RRM more than half lower), and RRM twins 1.4 percent. Panel B reads as how many times higher the adjusted odds of an early birth are with treatment versus untreated women: ART single-baby preterm about 3.3 times higher and low birth weight about 2.9 times higher versus never-treated women (Stanford 2016), and IVF preterm about 4.2 times higher and IUI preterm about 3.2 times higher versus untreated subfertile women (Sanders 2022). Panel A is a head-count percentage and Panel B is an adjusted-odds multiple; they are different measures on different scales and must not be compared directly." class="chart-light" loading="lazy" width="960" height="540" />
