              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/boyle-funnel.svg" alt="Funnel chart of the Boyle 2025 cohort: 249 couples had at least one RRM consultation, 187 committed to treatment (75 percent), 98 conceived (52 percent of treated), and 77 had a live birth (41 percent of treated)." class="chart-light" loading="lazy" width="760" height="470" />
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/boyle-funnel-dark.svg" alt="Funnel chart of the Boyle 2025 cohort: 249 couples had at least one RRM consultation, 187 committed to treatment (75 percent), 98 conceived (52 percent of treated), and 77 had a live birth (41 percent of treated)." class="chart-dark" loading="lazy" width="760" height="470" />
              <figcaption>Boyle et al. 2025 (2019 Dublin cohort): the patient funnel from first consultation to live birth. The conception (52%) and live-birth (41%) percentages are computed against the 187 couples who committed to treatment, not the 249 who only consulted.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <figure class="chart-figure">
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/boyle-reassignment.svg" alt="Paired before-and-after horizontal bars showing the percentage of the Boyle 2025 cohort carrying each diagnosis before versus after RRM evaluation: unexplained infertility falls from 24 to 1 percent, while ovulatory dysfunction rises 19 to 76, corpus luteum deficiency 0 to 71, clinical endorphin deficiency 1 to 67, hypoandrogenism 0 to 31, and endometritis 0 to 17 percent." class="chart-light" loading="lazy" width="820" height="580" />
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/boyle-reassignment-dark.svg" alt="Paired before-and-after horizontal bars showing the percentage of the Boyle 2025 cohort carrying each diagnosis before versus after RRM evaluation: unexplained infertility falls from 24 to 1 percent, while ovulatory dysfunction rises 19 to 76, corpus luteum deficiency 0 to 71, clinical endorphin deficiency 1 to 67, hypoandrogenism 0 to 31, and endometritis 0 to 17 percent." class="chart-dark" loading="lazy" width="820" height="580" />
              <figcaption>Boyle et al. 2025 (Table 4): the share of the cohort carrying each diagnosis before versus after the RRM work-up. Couples may carry several diagnoses, so the columns do not sum to 100%.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>The Sánchez-Méndez and colleagues 2025 study is the <a href="/library/natural-procreative-technology-naprotechnology-for-infertility-take-home-baby-ra-recv02qu0r8ycnzoa/">largest published NaProTechnology cohort to date</a>, enrolling 1,310 couples at a single center in Madrid over five years. The crude take-home-baby (live birth) rate was 35.3% (463 of 1,310). The adjusted-cumulative rate tracked upward over time: 20.2% at 9 months, 28.9% at 12 months, 39.0% at 18 months, 50.0% at 22-24 months, and a plateau of 62.1% from 31-33 months onward. This time-curve matters: couples or clinicians looking at a 12-month rate alone would see 28.9%, missing the additional pregnancies that accrued over the subsequent year and a half.</p>
            <p>The age gradient in this cohort is steep and clinically important. Adjusted-cumulative live-birth rates by age band: 83.7% for women aged 18-30, 63.2% for ages 31-35, 53.3% for ages 36-40, and 24.4% for women over 40. Age is the strongest predictor of outcome. The mean cohort age of 35.0 means the overall rate sits between the 31-35 and 36-40 age bands, and readers evaluating these numbers for their own situation should weight the age-specific figures accordingly.</p>
            <p>The cohort also enrolled 27.5% of couples with prior ART. Among that subgroup, the crude take-home-baby rate was 25.3%, still meaningful in a population that had already pursued ART without a live birth. And what was "unexplained" before workup became diagnosable: fewer than 2% of couples completed evaluation without receiving at least one specific diagnosis. The study does not report the full diagnostic reassignment table, but the near-elimination of the "unexplained" category across 1,310 couples documents what systematic evaluation finds when it is applied systematically.</p>

            <figure class="chart-figure">
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/sm-cumulative-curve.svg" alt="Step/line curve of adjusted cumulative take-home-baby (live birth) rate over 36 months from Sanchez-Mendez 2025: rising from 1.1 percent at 3 months to 50.0 percent at 24 months and plateauing at 62.1 percent from 31 to 33 months onward." class="chart-light" loading="lazy" width="760" height="470" />
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/sm-cumulative-curve-dark.svg" alt="Step/line curve of adjusted cumulative take-home-baby (live birth) rate over 36 months from Sanchez-Mendez 2025: rising from 1.1 percent at 3 months to 50.0 percent at 24 months and plateauing at 62.1 percent from 31 to 33 months onward." class="chart-dark" loading="lazy" width="760" height="470" />
              <figcaption>Sanchez-Mendez et al. 2025 (Table 3, adjusted cumulative): the cumulative take-home-baby rate by 3-month treatment band for the 1,310-couple cohort, reaching 50.0% at 24 months and plateauing at 62.1% from the 31-to-33-month band onward.</figcaption>
            </figure>

            <figure class="chart-figure">
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/sm-by-age.svg" alt="Vertical bars of adjusted cumulative take-home-baby (live birth) rate by maternal age band from Sanchez-Mendez 2025: 83.7 percent at 18-30, 63.2 percent at 31-35, 53.3 percent at 36-40, and 24.4 percent above 40." class="chart-light" loading="lazy" width="760" height="460" />
              <img src="/images/rrm-success-rates/sm-by-age-dark.svg" alt="Vertical bars of adjusted cumulative take-home-baby (live birth) rate by maternal age band from Sanchez-Mendez 2025: 83.7 percent at 18-30, 63.2 percent at 31-35, 53.3 percent at 36-40, and 24.4 percent above 40." class="chart-dark" loading="lazy" width="760" height="460" />
              <figcaption>Sanchez-Mendez et al. 2025 (Figure 2B, adjusted cumulative): adjusted cumulative take-home-baby rate by maternal age band for the 1,310-couple cohort, with a significant decline across bands (p&lt;0.001).</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>

          <section id="how-to-read-these-numbers">
            <SectionShare id="how-to-read-these-numbers" title="How to Read These Numbers" />
            <!-- T9: methodology explainer -- crude vs adjusted/cumulative LBR, conception vs live-birth, per-cycle vs per-patient denominators, preprint/lower-tier caveats, why outcomeType matters. -->

            <p><strong>Crude vs. adjusted-cumulative rates describe different things.</strong> A crude rate divides live births by all enrolled couples, treating every dropout as a non-live-birth. An adjusted-cumulative (life-table) rate censors withdrawals and estimates what fraction of couples would succeed given the full follow-up window. These can differ by 10 to 25 percentage points in the same cohort. They answer different questions. This page reports both and labels which is which.</p>
            <p><strong>Per-couple and per-cycle denominators measure different things.</strong> RRM studies predominantly report per-couple rates: one outcome per enrolled couple over a defined period. IVF registries predominantly report per-cycle or per-embryo-transferred rates: one outcome per individual treatment attempt. A couple who transfers three embryos across three IVF cycles has contributed three observations to the per-cycle rate and one observation to a per-couple rate. Comparing a 36-month per-couple RRM rate to a single-transfer-attempt IVF rate treats fundamentally different denominators as equivalent.</p>
            <p><strong>The published cohorts enroll a harder-to-treat population.</strong> Couples who seek RRM typically arrive with prior medical encounters, previous diagnoses, and often prior treatment failures. They are older and have been trying longer than couples pursuing first-line ART. This means the published RRM rates reflect what the approach achieves in a population that IVF benchmarks do not capture, and they understate what RRM achieves when applied first-line. The studies are honest about their populations, and that transparency is what makes the figures interpretable.</p>
            <p><strong>No randomized controlled trials have been published comparing RRM and IVF.</strong> The best available comparison is the <a href="/library/restorative-reproductive-medicine-rrm-outcomes-compared-to-in-vitro-fertilization-rec4qqhafqb8stlnd/">2025 Dublin retrospective cohort</a> benchmarked against contemporaneous SART and CDC registry data. The demand for RCTs is worth examining directly: IVF was adopted at scale and became the dominant approach to infertility before any randomized controlled trials existed comparing it to expectant management. A Cochrane review documented that only two small RCTs, 86 women total, ever examined IVF versus expectant management. The standard being applied to RRM was never applied to IVF. The appropriate conclusion is that both fields built their evidence base on observational data, and RRM's observational record is strong.</p>
            <p><strong>Journal tier matters, and the preprint flag matters.</strong> The studies in this table span journals from indexed general-audience family medicine to specialty journals to one preprint (the <a href="/library/pregnancy-outcomes-from-a-restorative-infertility-treatment-model-a-single-centr-916a9dda/">2021 Australian cohort by James and colleagues</a>). Preprints have not completed peer review. Lower-tier journals have less rigorous peer review. This page notes preprint and lower-tier status where applicable. These distinctions matter when weighing the evidence.</p>
            <p><strong>The ranges in the stat cards span genuinely different things.</strong> The 18.4% crude live-birth rate comes from a cohort where 100% of couples had prior failed IVF, average 2.1 cycles, mean age 37.2. The 73.6% adjusted-cumulative rate comes from a mixed cohort with broader inclusion criteria and 24 months of follow-up. The range reflects different populations over different periods using different metrics. Heterogeneous populations produce heterogeneous outcomes, and both anchors are accurately described.</p>
          </section>

